.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt TAP 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tap ,
.Nm tun
.Nd Ethernet interface whose other end is a descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/net/tun
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The character device
.Pa /dev/net/tun ,
10:200 as on Linux, makes an Ethernet interface whose wire is a file
descriptor: each frame the stack sends on the interface is one
.Xr read 2
of the descriptor, and each
.Xr write 2
or
.Xr writev 2
of the descriptor is one frame the interface receives.
QEMU's
.Ql -netdev tap
is its main user: it joins a guest's network card to an interface of the
host, so that the host routes through the guest or the guest through the
host.
.Pp
A program opens the device and gives the descriptor an interface with
.Dv TUNSETIFF ,
whose argument is a
.Vt struct ifreq
with the name in
.Va ifr_name
and the flags in
.Va ifr_flags .
The flags must be
.Dv IFF_TAP | IFF_NO_PI :
Ethernet frames with no packet information before them.
An empty name, or one that ends in
.Ql %d ,
takes the first free
.Li tapN ,
or the stem given and a digit; another name takes that interface when it
is a tap no descriptor holds, and makes it when no interface has the
name.
The call writes the name it gave back to
.Va ifr_name .
The interface starts down, with a random locally administered station
address and an MTU of 1500; it has a carrier while a descriptor holds it.
.Pp
A read returns one frame, whole, or its first bytes when the buffer is
shorter; a descriptor opened with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
returns
.Er EAGAIN
when no frame waits.
Each interface holds up to 1024 frames for its reader, as Linux's tun
queues 1000; a frame sent while the queue is full is dropped and counted
in the interface's transmit drops.
A write is one frame of 14 to 1514 bytes; a longer one is taken and
counted as dropped, so that the writer goes on.
.Xr poll 2
reports the descriptor readable while a frame waits and always
writable.
.Pp
When the last descriptor of the open closes, the interface loses its
carrier and its queued frames and, unless
.Dv TUNSETPERSIST
keeps it, is removed with its addresses, routes and neighbours.
.Ss Requests
.Bl -tag -width TUNGETVNETHDRSZ
.It Dv TUNGETFEATURES
Writes
.Dv IFF_TAP | IFF_NO_PI
to the
.Vt unsigned int
at the argument, the flags
.Dv TUNSETIFF
takes.
.It Dv TUNSETIFF
As above.
.It Dv TUNGETIFF
Writes the interface's name and flags,
.Dv IFF_PERSIST
among them when set.
.It Dv TUNSETPERSIST
A nonzero argument keeps the interface after its descriptor closes.
.It Dv TUNSETOFFLOAD
Takes 0, no offload; any other flag is
.Er EINVAL .
.It Dv TUNGETSNDBUF
Writes
.Dv INT_MAX ,
Linux's default.
.It Dv TUNSETSNDBUF , TUNSETOWNER , TUNSETGROUP , TUNSETLINK , TUNSETNOCSUM , TUNSETDEBUG
Taken and ignored.
.It Dv TUNSETVNETHDRSZ , TUNGETVNETHDRSZ , TUNSETVNETLE , TUNGETVNETLE , TUNSETVNETBE , TUNGETVNETBE , TUNSETQUEUE
.Er EINVAL :
there is no virtio header and one queue.
.El
.Sh LIMITS
Two tap interfaces at once, among the four interfaces the kernel has
besides
.Li lo .
Only the TAP form: no TUN interface of IP packets, no packet information
header, no virtio header and so no offload, and one queue.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/net/tun
.It Pa /dev/net/tun
The device.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Give a QEMU guest the interface
.Li tap0
and route to it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# qemu-system-x86_64 -netdev tap,id=n0,ifname=tap0,script=no,downscript=no \e
    -device virtio-net-pci,netdev=n0 ...
# ifconfig tap0 192.168.100.1 netmask 255.255.255.0 up
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Er EBADFD
A read, write or request that needs the interface came before
.Dv TUNSETIFF ,
or the descriptor's interface is gone.
.It Er EBUSY
.Dv TUNSETIFF
named an interface another descriptor holds.
.It Er EINVAL
.Dv TUNSETIFF
on a descriptor that has an interface, with flags other than
.Dv IFF_TAP | IFF_NO_PI ,
a name too long, or the name of an interface that is no tap; a write
shorter than an Ethernet header.
.It Er ENFILE
No digit is free for the name's stem.
.It Er ENOSPC
Every interface of the kernel's, or both tap records, are taken.
.It Er ENOTTY
An unknown request.
.It Er EPERM
.Dv TUNSETIFF
without
.Dv CAP_NET_ADMIN .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ifconfig 1 ,
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr kvm 4 ,
.Xr virtio-net 4
.Sh HISTORY
The device follows Linux's drivers/net/tun.c and the numbers of
linux/if_tun.h.
